t was put into a warm bath tor twenty minutes, and then carefully returned to bed. Aconite 3X was administered every half-hour, and continued hourly for a day or two afterward until he was somewhat relieved. CraUeguSf five-drop doses of the tincture, was then administered five times daily for a long time. The effects of this remedy were most remarkable; the cardiac irritation gradually lessened; the area of dulness decreased and the rhythm improved; at the same time all the general symptoms improved rapidly. He has now t>een using the remedy for several months, and the result is most satisfactory. I have every reason to expect a cure of the extreme symptoms, and believe the heart virill be reduced to a safe hypertrophv, which will virtually be a cure." A girl of twelve had a sudden collapse in third week of typhoid (Cliniquc, vii. 52). Crakeg. gtt. V. every two hours was given on these indications: Great pallor, irregular breathing, cold extremities, pulse 120, very weak and irregular. Sitychniaj Digit, and CacL had failed to give more than temporary reuef. Under Cratceg. she rallied at once. Cases of hypertrophy with failing power, dilatation, alcoholic heart, and nervous palpitation have all been relieved by the remedy. T. C. Duncan (/7. Recorder, xii. 199) gave complete relief in a case of angina pectoris, the pain being above and to left of stomach; pulse strong and forcible; indications of hypertrophy were found. A very tender spot on left side of spine. Craiagus removed at the same time << a hurried, flurried feeling" and the anxious expression of her tanc^. Dr. Duncan produced in himself when proving Cratc^us a '' flurried feeling," which he attributed to the rapid action of the stimulated heart. A nervous lady medical student who also proved it had " an unusual rush of blood to the head with confused feeling," followed later by '' a feeling of quiet and calmness mentally." The remedy is well worth a thorough proving.

