ning gr. 12J of Morphine placed in the middle of a baked bean with suicidal intent. It was in the evening, having fasted since noorf. She went to sleep and woke at 7 a.m, next morning, surprisea to find herself in this world. She slept again till ii a.m., when she had to get up, but could not walk. A doctor was sent for. She vomited a little mucus, some dark specks like blood, and a small piece of lettuce, eaten the previous noon. Query: Did Phas, antidote Morph, f ii2) Robust man, 50, had grippe, which developed rheumatism, worse in arms, occasionally below elbows. Pain so severe he could not lie in bed at nights. Drank much water. Passed much urine, which Cushing found to contain 3*5 per cent, of sugar. Phas, sx every four hours removed all but a trace of sugar in eight days, and ’the patient was in other respects well. S. R. Stone {Amer, Hom,^ xxiv. 123) reports the case of Mr. T., 69, whom he found semi-conscious, yet suffering severely, having been seized with distress in heart region; respiration laboured; pulse 51. Had had attacks previously, but lighter, and the pulse in them had alyirays been slow. Phas, ox was given, at first every half-hour, then every hour. Next day patient wa» nearly well, and said he could feel every dose working.^^ In a case reported by Cushing, a nurse, 50, had fearful time with her heart palpitating and feeling as if she should die Phas, isx cured, PaPltent ^ never took anything in her life that did her so much good (ff. R., xii. 237). Remarkable symptoms of the proving were: Soreness foncA-^yeballs, right rib, epigastrium, right humerus. The headache was < by movement of the head or any mental exertion, reading, writing, &c. Pressure <. Breathing slow; pulse slow or extinct.

