Sabal Serrulata.

Serenoa serrulata. Saw Palmetto. (Sandy soils of sea coast South Carolina to Florida; most luxuriant nearest the sea.) N, 0 . Palmaceae. (i) Tincture of fresh ripe berries and seeds, all crushed and macerated in 90 per cent, alcohol for fourteen days and decanted (Hale). (2) Fluid Extract, probably four times stronger than the tincture^’ (Hale). [C. S. Estep (Affi, Hom.j xxvi. 133) uses a dark-green Fluid Extract which is made from the fresh green berries, and is “ almost as green as grass and has an oily surface.’' He makes his dilutions from this. Other preparations have disappointed him.] (3) Oil (prepared from the expressed juice of the friiit^y allowing it to stand a few days). (4) Saccharated Oil (one part of the oil to seven parts of cane sugar). (5) Malted Sabal (one part of the Oil to seven of Maltine). (6) Aqua Olei Sabal (a preparation suggested by Haje made by triturating gtt. xvi. of the Oil with Mag. carb. 5j, gradually adding a pint of distilled water and decanting. For use as a spray).

