Characteristics. — Santonin, is the favourite anthelmintic of the old school, and it is chiefiy against lumbrici that it has been given. From 2 to 5 grains are the ordinary doses, but these have caused severe and, in one or two in.stances, fatal poisoning — convulsions, left-side paralysis, delirium, vomiting, and purging have resulted. A case recorded by Demme was quoted in Bnt. Med. 7 ^.. March 26, 1892: A boy^ 3, was given by his mother during three days fifteen tablets, each containing gr. of Santonin. 'The symptoms produced were: Vomiting, dilatation of llic pupils, collapse, cyanosis, dyspnoea, and finally convulsions. After a warm bath with cold affusions to head and spine, consciousness returned. The temperature was i03'5"’, epistaxis and hainioglobinuna occurred, and a scarlatiiia-like eruption was noticed. The child ultimately recovered. After a dose of 7 centigrammes, a child, 2, was seized with prostation, convulsions, cramps, and icterus, followed on the third day by salivation and ulceration of the gum {Bull. Thcr. Jaii. 30, 1872). Less serious but very characteristic are the effects 011 the senses, especially the sense of sight. Visions of figures have been produced, and coloured vision is a frequent phenomenon, even when the lower triturations have been used. Yellow is the predominant colour, but green and violet vision are also marked. The urine also is yellow coloured and stains yellow, and is passed with much urging and painful burning. A kind of giant urticaria occurred in one case. Hale records that Sant, has cured cases of “ neiwous blindness.*’ It was given to an old man, quite blind, for worms, and his sight partially returned. This led to further trials. It was given in nine cases of cataract, and, according to Hale, four were cured, the rest not benefited. Hale mentions enuresis, strangury, chronic cystitis, as having been cured by it. It has made some “ brilliant cures of chronic catarrh of the bladder. Infantile remittents, with or withou